  "explanation": [
    "The orders since 1 January 2026 are 27,464 of 100,000, over a quarter. PostgreSQL expected exactly that many and still read them through orders_created_at_idx. The table is stored in created_at order, so the matches sit next to each other and the index reads them in order, at 1 a row instead of 4: 27,464, against 100,000 for reading every order. On a table stored in another order, the same index scan would cost 4 × 27,464 = 109,856. How many rows is too many for an index depends on how the table is stored."
